yes(449,'D','Microorganisms produce organic acids during continued palm-wine fermentation. For example, acetic-acid bacteria can oxidise ethanol to ethanoic acid, increasing acidity and producing a sour taste.',['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11337016/'],question='Palm wine turns sour with time because',options=['Its sugar content is converted into alcohol','The carbon dioxide formed during fermentation has a sour taste','It is commonly adulterated by tappers and sellers','Microbial activity produces organic acids in it'],audit='Removed spillover from 1993Q48 and page40 marker from D using PDF39.')
yes(450,'B','CH₄ has molar mass 16 g mol⁻¹, so 8 g contains 0.5 mol. CH₃CH₂CH₃ has molar mass 44 g mol⁻¹, so 22 g also contains 0.5 mol. Equal amounts of gas occupy equal volumes at the same temperature and pressure: 11.2 dm³.',[GAS,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13510?exam_type=jamb&exam_year=1994'],question='8 g of CH₄ occupies 11.2 dm³ at s.t.p. What volume would 22 g of CH₃CH₂CH₃ occupy under the same conditions? [C = 12, H = 1]',options=['3.7 dm³','11.2 dm³','22.4 dm³','33.6 dm³'],audit='Matching Myschool1994 question explicitly restores terminal CH₃. Independent mole calculation corrects old D to B; no factor-of-three multiplication.',check='gas-moles')
yes(451,'D','For a fixed amount of gas, PV/T is constant. Doubling both pressure and volume multiplies PV by four, so the absolute temperature must become 4 × 273 = 1092 K.',[GAS],question='To what temperature must a fixed amount of gas initially at 273 K be heated to double both its volume and pressure?',options=['298 K','546 K','819 K','1092 K'],check='combined-gas-law')
yes(452,'B','Vapour density relative to hydrogen compares the masses of equal gas volumes at the same temperature and pressure. Since hydrogen has relative molecular mass 2, Mr = 2 × vapour density. Thus Y is the vapour density.',[REB],question='A gas has relative molecular mass 2Y. Using the hydrogen-reference convention for vapour density, what is Y?',audit='Made traditional hydrogen-reference convention explicit; no changed measured quantity.')
yes(453,'C','By Graham’s law, diffusion rate is inversely proportional to the square root of gas density under the same conditions. Hence rate(X)/rate(Y) = √(2.0/0.5) = √4 = 2.0.',[GAS],question='At the same temperature and pressure, gases X and Y have densities 0.5 g dm⁻³ and 2.0 g dm⁻³ respectively. What is the rate of diffusion of X relative to Y?',check='graham-ratio')
yes(454,'B','Heating increases the average kinetic energy of the gas molecules. In a fixed-volume container they strike its walls more frequently and with greater momentum, increasing the pressure.',[GAS],question='Heating a gas in a fixed-volume container increases its pressure because',options=['It decreases the number of collisions between molecules','The gas molecules bombard the container walls more frequently','It increases the number of collisions between molecules','It causes the molecules to combine'],audit='Removed stray curves word and made fixed-volume convention explicit. Wall collisions explain pressure; intermolecular collisions alone do not.')
